1
00:00:00,721 --> 00:00:02,509
This is Lore.

2
00:00:02,508 --> 00:00:07,113
Every open-source project carries knowledge that nobody wrote down:

3
00:00:07,163 --> 00:00:12,671
who knows the query planner, why a limit exists, who should look at a new issue.

4
00:00:12,721 --> 00:00:17,405
Lore reads a project's public GitHub history into FalkorDB,

5
00:00:17,455 --> 00:00:19,571
and answers from the graph.

6
00:00:20,897 --> 00:00:25,845
Here it's loaded with FalkorDB's own GitHub organisation: six repos,

7
00:00:25,895 --> 00:00:30,623
about a hundred and fifty people, thirteen hundred pull requests,

8
00:00:30,673 --> 00:00:32,535
refreshed every six hours.

9
00:00:32,534 --> 00:00:34,439
I'm signed in with GitHub,

10
00:00:34,489 --> 00:00:38,572
so Lore starts by recalling what it remembers about me.

11
00:00:38,622 --> 00:00:41,972
Now I ask who knows the query planner.

12
00:00:42,022 --> 00:00:46,405
On the right, Under the hood shows every step as it happens:

13
00:00:46,455 --> 00:00:51,208
the tool Gemini picks, then the Cypher query it runs on FalkorDB,

14
00:00:51,258 --> 00:00:52,760
with rows and timing.

15
00:00:53,615 --> 00:00:57,665
Every name comes with the pull requests that prove it.

16
00:00:57,665 --> 00:01:03,399
Proof draws that path in the graph: a person, the PRs they wrote or reviewed,

17
00:01:03,449 --> 00:01:05,278
and the area of the code.

18
00:01:05,328 --> 00:01:10,297
The score behind it is computed inside FalkorDB, from merged PRs,

19
00:01:10,347 --> 00:01:14,390
reviews and comments, with recent work counting more.

20
00:01:15,316 --> 00:01:18,329
Triage takes a real open issue.

21
00:01:18,329 --> 00:01:21,838
Lore works out which areas of the code it touches,

22
00:01:21,888 --> 00:01:24,970
finds related past issues and pull requests,

23
00:01:25,020 --> 00:01:28,316
and picks an active person who knows that code.

24
00:01:28,366 --> 00:01:31,541
Then it drafts a reply with sources.

25
00:01:31,591 --> 00:01:35,516
It's a dry run: Lore never posts anywhere.

26
00:01:41,498 --> 00:01:45,648
Each signed-in person gets a memory graph of their own.

27
00:01:45,648 --> 00:01:51,113
Lore remembers what I asked and the areas I care about, and when I come back,

28
00:01:51,163 --> 00:01:56,486
it starts with the pull requests merged in those areas since my last visit.

29
00:01:56,536 --> 00:01:59,298
One click forgets everything.

30
00:02:00,226 --> 00:02:04,451
Teams runs label propagation over who reviews whom.

31
00:02:04,451 --> 00:02:10,050
On FalkorDB it finds the core database group and two groups on the browser app,

32
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:13,626
with PageRank showing who holds each one together.

33
00:02:13,676 --> 00:02:19,507
Knowledge risk lists areas with recent work that only one active person knows

34
00:02:19,557 --> 00:02:19,888
well.

35
00:02:21,673 --> 00:02:23,510
Does it work?

36
00:02:23,511 --> 00:02:27,578
We backtested on three hundred real FalkorDB pull requests,

37
00:02:27,628 --> 00:02:31,835
rebuilding the graph as it stood the day each one was opened.

38
00:02:31,886 --> 00:02:36,636
Lore had a real reviewer in its top three ninety percent of the time,

39
00:02:36,686 --> 00:02:38,723
ahead of the simple baselines.

40
00:02:38,773 --> 00:02:44,210
The README has the graph model, every Cypher query, and the limits.

41
00:02:45,094 --> 00:02:50,977
Lore is open source, runs on FalkorDB, and works on any public project:

42
00:02:51,027 --> 00:02:54,069
sign in with GitHub and paste a repo.

43
00:02:54,069 --> 00:02:56,119
Thanks for watching.
